        "For int data[3], the name data converts to a pointer to the first element in most expressions. int* p=data; and int* end=data+3; define a half-open range. ++p moves to the next element of that array, not an arbitrary byte. While p!=end, you may read *p. Once p==end, reading is invalid.\n\nA raw pointer does not contain the array's size. A function int sum(const int* data,int size) must receive the length separately and respect its contract. For new interfaces, std::vector or std::array is usually clearer; studying pointers does not mean recommending them as replacements for containers. You must not subtract pointers to unrelated objects to obtain a useful distance.\n\nAn array type and a pointer type are different things: in its declaration scope, int data[5] makes sizeof(data)/sizeof(data[0]) yield the element count, but the same calculation for a parameter int* data is meaningless. void* stores an address without an element type and can be dereferenced only after an explicit cast to a concrete type. A pointer to a pointer T** stores the address of another pointer: this is how arrays of pointers to strings in dynamic memory are built, a technique covered in the dynamic storage section.",
